{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>A holy linen tunic he shall put on, and linen breeches shall be upon his flesh, and with a linen sash he shall gird himself, and with a linen turban.</b> Scripture mentions four garments that are of linen (בד), and it mentions 'linen' with each one of them. And in Torat Kohanim they said: 'בד — to exclude the breastpiece; בד — to exclude the ephod; בד — to exclude the robe; בד — to exclude the diadem (ציץ).' And the reason is that the garments of the High Priest are eight: four of them are linen, and four of them contain gold. And he was not permitted to enter the innermost sanctuary (לפני ולפנים) in garments of gold, because a prosecutor does not become a defender (אין קטיגור נעשה סניגור). Therefore it mentioned 'linen' with each one of them, to exclude the other garments in which 'לפני ה׳' is written — for it is said of the breastpiece (Exodus 28:29) ״לפני ה׳ תמיד״, and it is said of the ephod (ibid.) ״לפני ה׳״, and it is said of the robe (ibid.) ״בבואו אל הקדש לפני ה׳״, and it is said of the diadem (ibid. v. 20) ״לרצון להם לפני ה׳״ — one might think he may enter with them into the innermost sanctuary; therefore it says 'בד,' to exclude the breastpiece; 'בד,' to exclude the ephod; 'בד,' to exclude the robe; 'בד,' to exclude the diadem. And it says: 'holy garments' — to teach that the High Priest who serves in the innermost sanctuary in the Temple below is a model (דוגמא) of the service performed in the Temple above. And his serving there in four garments is a model of the four camps of the Shekhinah that minister before Him on high, or a model of the holy living creatures (חיות הקדש) with four wings and four faces. And Scripture should have said 'holy garments' (בגדים קדושים), but it said 'בגדי קדש' — to explain that they are a model, and they correspond to 'קדש.' And this is what you find that they said in Leviticus Rabbah: 'As the service above, so the service below; as in the service above one man is among them dressed in linen (בדים), so in the service below — ״כתנת בד קדש ילבש״,' end quote. And the one dressed in linen is Michael. And the intention is that the priest was not qualified to enter the innermost sanctuary, the House of Mercies, except in linen garments." "<b>And if</b> you look closely at the language of בד, you will find it embraces several wondrous intentions: for it alludes to humility and lowliness, and it alludes to unity, and by its color to forgiveness, and by its number to the middle line. It alludes to humility, for linen (the flax plant) grows rising from the dust, for the humbling of the worshiper, who is dust from the earth — unlike wool, which comes from living creatures. And so they said in tractate Yoma: 'a thing that grows from the ground, בד בבד.' And this is the language of בדים and branches. It alludes to unity, for the word בד means 'single' (יחידי). And it is known that its thread is single and cannot be split at all, unlike wool, whose thread can be split, and likewise hair in man. And this is what it says: 'בד בבד' — that is, single and indivisible; if so, it bears a sign and allusion to unity. And likewise בד is white, and the color white is a sign of forgiveness. And Rashi, of blessed memory, explained there in tractate Yoma, at the beginning of chapter 'Ba Lo': 'בד — a thing that grows from the ground is flax (פשתן), which comes only from the central stalk, unlike canvas (קנבוס) which grows on the outside around the stalk.' And behold, the numerical value of בד is six, and likewise בד is called in Scripture שש (six) — an allusion to the middle line alluded to in the vav (ו) of the Name, of which the priest is a model." "<b>And already</b> you know that the cherubim in the innermost sanctuary are alluded to in the letters heh and vav (ה\"א וא\"ו). And behold, the heh is included within the vav. And this is the language of בד, with which he enters the innermost sanctuary — for its numerical value is six (שש)." "<b>And further,</b> another matter appears to me in this verse, and it is that it is known that the parts of the human body are three, corresponding to the three parts of existence. This is to indicate that he encompasses all the upper and lower realms. From this he has the power to draw down blessing and to channel supernal force into the lower realms — and this is the High Priest designated for the Temple service. And this is what it says: ״ומכנסי בד יהיו על בשרו״ — and this is from his waist downward, which is the lower part of man, corresponding to the lower world. ״ובאבנט בד יחגור״ — this is the middle of the body, corresponding to the middle world, and it corresponds to the realm of the spheres (עולם הגלגלים). ״ובמצנפת בד יצנוף״ — the manner of the turban is to place it on his head, and the head is the uppermost part of man, and it corresponds to the world of the angels, which is higher and more exalted than the rest. And behold, the order of donning is from below upward, and the thought of the priest ranges through the three parts of existence from below upward, in the order of putting on the garments, and is sanctified by the sanctity of the supernal realms. And this is why Scripture began with קדש and concluded with קדש."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:10.929039208Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}